\section{Checks}{

What each check means and what to do about it, in the words it is printed with. A reference supports what was found and why; a remedy draws on it only where it says so, and anything marked as limn's own reasoning is not the source's:
\itemize{
\item \strong{few origins} (warn): The comparison has T origins over several horizons, fewer than about 100. With so few origins the usual Diebold-Mariano test rejects too often when forecasts overlap: in limn's simulation, up to 12\% of the time at 5\% with 50 origins or fewer (studies/findings/compare_dm_fixed.md). Reference: Coroneo and Iacone (2020), Journal of Applied Econometrics 35(4), 391-409, section 4.2 (the test) and section 5 (its bandwidth). Use method = "wpe", the reference's fixed-smoothing test, which held its size from 10 origins up in the same simulation; or compare over more origins.
\item \strong{Diebold-Mariano variance} (warn): The long-run variance truncated at lag L was negative, so it was taken as 0 and the null hypothesis of equal accuracy was rejected. A long-run variance truncated with equal weights can come out negative. Diebold and Mariano's rule for that case is to take it as 0 and reject the null, so the statistic is infinite and the p-value 0. They call a negative estimate a rare event. In limn's own check against the simulation design of Harvey, Leybourne and Newbold (1997, p. 284), it arose in 35\% of samples with 8 origins at horizon 5 (lag 4), and in 17\% with 16, and the rule took the modified test's size at nominal 10\% to 41.9\% there, and to 29.7\% with 8 origins at horizon 3 (lag 2), against their Table 1's 12.9\% and 18.1\% (p. 285) (studies/findings/compare_dm_negative.md). Reference: Diebold and Mariano (1995), Journal of Business and Economic Statistics 13, 253-263, section 1.1. Read this rejection with care, or use method = "wpe", the default, which needs no lag and held its size from 10 origins up in limn's simulation.
}
}

\section{Catching the checks}{

Each check above is raised as a warning of class \verb{lmn_<id>}, beside
\code{lmn_diagnostic}, and is kept on the result as a row of \code{attr(x, "checks")},
a data frame whose \code{id} column names it:
\itemize{
\item \code{dm_few_origins} (warning class \code{lmn_dm_few_origins}): few origins over
several horizons for the usual test;
\item \code{dm_negative_variance} (warning class \code{lmn_dm_negative_variance}): the
truncated long-run variance was negative, so it was taken as 0 and the
null hypothesis rejected.
}

So \code{withCallingHandlers(ilm_compare(...), lmn_dm_negative_variance = function(w) ...)} handles that warning alone, and \code{attr(cmp, "checks")$id} says which checks
a comparison raised.
}

